  <info>## WHAT IS IT?

This model allows for the exploration of learning in bandit problems with individuals implementing variations on the epsilon-greedy learning rule.  The model allow one to explore how individuals learn on their own as well as in groups structured in different types of network structures.

## HOW IT WORKS

The underlying learning problem is a 2- to 4-armed bandit problem.  Each bandit is characterized by a mean payoff and variance.  A single pull of a bandit arm returns a value drawn from a normal distribution with those parameters.  

Individuals start off with some initial sample from each arm (specified by parameter INITIAL-SAMPLE).  They then, with probability 1-EPSILON, pull the arm that has performed best among the results that they have seen.  With probability EPSILON, they pull from among all the bandits uniformly at random.

Agents reevaluate their choice of arm after a set number of rounds to determine if another does better.  This is specified with the REEVAL-FREQUENCY paramater.  Agents continue for a set number of rounds, specified in the NUMBER-OF-PULLS parameter at which point the simulation stops.

All individuals can "see" their own results from each pull of the bandit. If the simulation involves more than one individual, they can be arranged in different network configurations.  In this case, individuals also see the results of others to whom they have a connection.  This model only features undirected networks, where results are transmitted in both directions.

## HOW TO USE IT

To run the model click the SETUP BUTTON. STEP has all players choose and pull one arm.  The GO button runs the simulation until all agents have pulled up to NUMBER-OF-PULLS.

The two plots illustrate the performance of the agents.  The top plot indicates how each bandit has done in the observed trials.  Once all agents abandon a particular bandit, you will notice that the plot is flat because no further observations are made of that bandit.  

The bottom plot shows how the community of agents is peforming, averaging over all bandits.  This represents a measure of success for the community.</info>
